# macOS
.DS_Store

# Xcode
build/
DerivedData/
*.xcuserstate
xcuserdata/
*.xccheckout
*.moved-aside
*.xcscmblueprint
*.hmap
*.ipa
*.dSYM.zip
*.dSYM

# Swift Package Manager
.swiftpm/
Package.resolved

# Files Claude delivers into the repo root. They are untracked by design, and
# without these lines a repo-wide `git clean -fd` deletes them along with any
# source data sitting beside them. It has happened once: v0.15's own tarball,
# the AT tax workbooks and app-concept.md all went in a single clean.
*.tar.gz
*.patch
*.bundle
# Source data kept beside the project, never committed but never disposable.
*.xlsx
app-concept.md

# Everything that is not the app. Delivered tarballs and patches, the source
# workbooks the datasets were generated from, and dead directories that can be
# looked at but not deleted from here.
_archive/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Things that must never be committed, whether or not they exist right now.
#
# v1.1a emptied the working folder: the payslips, Config/, functions/, the two
# duplicate scripts and the probe's binary were all deleted, and HANDOFF.md was
# moved into _archive/. These rules stay anyway, because every one of them
# describes something that comes BACK. A payslip gets dropped in to run the
# probe, `git checkout pool-backend` restores Config/ and functions/, and the
# probe rebuilds .build/ on demand. A rule that only holds while the folder
# happens to be empty is not protection.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Real payslips. Somebody's actual pay, with names, employers, tax numbers and
# salaries on them, and the only thing tools/payslip_probe can be pointed at.
# Once a document like this is in a commit it is in the history for good, so
# this rule matters more than anything else in this file. The reader's own
# fixtures live in the Swift source, so a fresh clone loses the ability to run
# the probe and none of the checks that gate a release.
payslip_examples/

# Left behind by a pool-backend checkout. Both are tracked on that branch, which
# is master plus exactly the Firebase backend, so committing either here
# reintroduces it piecemeal. functions/ is also ~234MB, nearly all node_modules.
# Config/Local.xcconfig is the real config rather than the .example, and its own
# header always claimed this name was gitignored; now it is.
Config/
functions/

# Byte-identical copies of the tracked tools/ scripts. Two copies of a script
# drift, and the repo root doubles as a working folder, so if one reappears it
# stays out of the history. `cp tools/<name> .` if one is ever wanted back.
/derive_dataset.py
/parse_qp2024.py

# tools/payslip_probe build output.
.build/

# The degraded images tools/payslip_corpus generates. Regenerable exactly, by
# design: the noise is seeded, so `payslip_corpus photos` produces byte-identical
# files every run. 144 JPEGs is about 45MB, and a corpus you can rebuild in a
# minute does not belong in the history. The PDFs and the truth files beside them
# ARE committed, so a fresh clone can run the harness without Chrome.
docs/fixtures/corpus/photos/
docs/fixtures/corpus/photos.json

# Agent tooling. CLAUDE.md is the internal operating manual for this repo and
# .claude/ is the permission config that goes with it. Both stay on disk so the
# tooling keeps working, and neither belongs in a public repository: one is a
# candid list of what is still wrong with the app, the other is not app source.
CLAUDE.md
.claude/
